        "tool_use_id": "call_9002f13dd2594b60bc3632d4",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"Why write code in 2026\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion on the role of manual coding in 2026. Covers debates on whether developers should still write code manually or rely entirely on AI agents. Key themes include: (1) Writing code helps build mental models and understanding - if you can't write code, you can't review it effectively. (2) Claude's hourly rate is ~$17, comparable to some human workers, but may rise as human engineers are 'Ubered away'. (3) LLMs can write complex code like Prolog interpreters in WebAssembly successfully. (4) AI tools compress development timelines but don't eliminate the need for developers who understand fundamentals. (5) The distinction between 'software prompters' who use AI for everything vs developers who use AI as a tool.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"softwaredoug (author), HN community\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"TechMedia | Hacker News\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48861923\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-25\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding\", \"human vs AI\", \"code review\", \"LLM limitations\", \"software engineering\"],\n    10→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    11→  },\n    12→  {\n    13→    \"title\": \"Large Language Models at the Intersection of Software Engineering and Software Security\",\n    14→    \"description\": \"August 24, 2026 arXiv paper presenting an evidence-centered structured survey and research agenda on LLMs in software engineering and security. The paper provides a comprehensive framework for understanding how LLMs are transforming both software development practices and security considerations.\",\n    15→    \"author\": \"Wei Lin, Tao Zhou, Zhaofei Xie, Changgui Hong\",\n    16→    \"source\": \"Technical | ArXiv\",\n    17→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21107\",\n    18→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-24\",\n    19→    \"topics\": [\"LLM security\", \"software engineering\", \"survey\", \"research agenda\", \"SE security intersection\"],\n    20→    \"type\": \"research\"\n    21→  },\n    22→  {\n    23→    \"title\": \"TRACE: Agentic Catalog Enrichment with Multi-source Evidence Grounding\",\n    24→    \"description\": \"August 24, 2026 arXiv paper on agentic systems for catalog enrichment using multi-source evidence grounding. Demonstrates advanced agentic AI capabilities for organizing and enriching technical content - relevant to AI coding documentation and knowledge management.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"Rohan Kumar, Steven Xu, Kyle MacDonald, Matthew Long, Bernice Chow, Mac VanRenterghem, Sudeep Das\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"Technical | ArXiv\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20844\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-24\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"agentic systems\", \"evidence grounding\", \"catalog enrichment\", \"multi-source AI\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"research\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"Structure for Reading, Prose for Writing: Asymmetric Structural Conditioning in Multi-Agent Document Authoring\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"August 24, 2026 arXiv paper on multi-agent document authoring with asymmetric structural conditioning. Relevant to AI coding documentation generation and how multiple AI agents can collaborate on complex writing tasks with different structural constraints.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Cheng Yu, Nikhil Mathew, Zhengjie Wang\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"Technical | ArXiv\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20786\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-24\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"multi-agent systems\", \"document authoring\", \"asymmetric conditioning\", \"AI collaboration\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"research\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"Coding agents have replaced every framework I used\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion on replacing frameworks with AI-generated code. Key insights: (1) AWS demo at re:Invent showed SRE agents autonomously detecting, triaging, and fixing bugs in 2 minutes. (2) Debate over whether agents can maintain complex systems without human understanding of 'mental models'. (3) Concern about 'vibe coding' leading to unmaintainable codebases when AI hits limitations. (4) AI productivity gains: some report 10x improvements, others minimal benefit. (5) Frameworks provide shared mental models and patterns that AI-generated bespoke code lacks. (6) Real-world experiment: team used prompts to replace legacy system but abandoned it after months due to complexity and token costs. (7) Determinism argument: compilers are deterministic, LLMs are not - you can't reliably verify AI code without understanding it.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"alainrk (author), HN community\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"TechMedia | Hacker News\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46923543\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-24\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"AI agents\", \"frameworks\", \"code generation\", \"software architecture\", \"maintainability\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"Show HN: Huzzah – A Novel Approach to Coding with AI\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion highlighting a novel approach to AI coding. Key insight: 'The great thing about AI coding is that you can go deep and detailed in hard sections and just plain ignore the simple stuff'. This reflects the emerging pattern of selective AI assistance - using AI for complex challenges while skipping boilerplate.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"HN community\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"TechMedia | Hacker News\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49378768\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-21\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding tools\", \"selective assistance\", \"productivity patterns\", \"Huzzah\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"LLMs Reward Expertise\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion about CLI coding tools including Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi and their different capabilities. Key insight: LLM tools are most effective when used by experts who know what to ask for - they reward existing expertise rather than replacing it.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"HN community\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"TechMedia | Hacker News\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49161518\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-19\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"CLI tools\", \"AI coding expertise\", \"Claude Code\", \"Codex\", \"OpenCode\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"AI Coding Agents: Adoption Trends - The JetBrains Blog\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"JetBrains Developer Ecosystem Survey 2026 findings on AI coding agent adoption. Key insights: (1) 90% of professional developers use AI coding agents at least weekly, with 68% using them daily. (2) Claude Code has become the most widely adopted tool at 39% of developers (up from 18% in January 2026), with 47% adoption in the US. (3) Claude Code has a 31% 'main tool' rate, indicating ~80% conversion from usage to primary tool. (4) Codex adoption grew 5x from 3% to 16%, with awareness jumping from 27% to 65%. (5) GitHub Copilot lost leadership, declining from 29% to 21% adoption. (6) Cursor declined from 18% to 12%, with significant drop in China (28% to 16%). (7) OpenCode reached 7% adoption with 42% mindshare despite no big company backing. (8) JetBrains AI adoption at 9%, with Claude Agent, Codex, GitHub Copilot, and OpenCode integrated into JetBrains IDEs.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Mikhail Bogdanov, JetBrains Research\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"TechMedia | JetBrains Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2026/08/ai-coding-agent-adoption-2026/\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-18\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding agents\", \"adoption statistics\", \"developer survey\", \"Claude Code\", \"Codex\", \"GitHub Copilot\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"research\"\n    81→  }\n    82→]\n    83→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
  },
